WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.474 --> 00:00:02.641
(tapping)

00:00:03.774 --> 00:00:05.616
- It's not every day that
you'll see an amputee

00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:07.733
walking along with a peg leg,

00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:10.066
and then begin to tap dance.

00:00:20.962 --> 00:00:22.148
My name is Evan Ruggiero,

00:00:22.148 --> 00:00:24.386
and I am a one-legged tap dancer.

00:00:24.386 --> 00:00:25.371
It's fun!

00:00:25.371 --> 00:00:28.668
I started tap dancing
when I was six-years-old.

00:00:28.668 --> 00:00:30.462
It's something that I fell in love with.

00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:32.275
I knew that tap dancing
would always be something

00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:36.170
that I wanted to do for
the rest of my life.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.837
When I was 19-years-old,

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.254
I had a pain in my right leg.

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:44.375
I was diagnosed with
osteosarcoma, bone cancer.

00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:46.208
And on May 21st, 2010,

00:00:47.766 --> 00:00:51.433
I had my right leg
amputated above-the-knee.

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.684
I was devastated.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.227
I just thought: how could a tap dancer

00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:01.227
be diagnosed with cancer in his leg?

00:01:03.192 --> 00:01:05.627
I remember my tap teacher
showing me footage

00:01:05.627 --> 00:01:07.564
of Peg Leg Bates,

00:01:07.564 --> 00:01:10.078
who was a great tap dancer
way back in the day.

00:01:10.078 --> 00:01:12.483
You know, had it not been for that footage,

00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:16.545
I would've never thought
that I could tap dance again.

00:01:16.545 --> 00:01:19.163
(tapping)

00:01:19.163 --> 00:01:21.857
When I put that peg leg
on for the first time,

00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:23.987
and I started making those rhythms,

00:01:23.987 --> 00:01:25.801
I felt a part of me that came back

00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:28.293
and I felt like my old self.

00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:29.126
Woo!

00:01:31.297 --> 00:01:34.221
I was able to teach
myself how to dance again.

00:01:34.221 --> 00:01:36.180
I just let muscle memory kick back in,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.361
and I tried not to think about making

00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:40.731
a step-hop-step with my peg leg,

00:01:40.731 --> 00:01:42.330
but just peg-hop-peg,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:46.247
and changing around the
vocabulary of the step.

00:01:47.087 --> 00:01:49.618
I perform in New York City.

00:01:49.618 --> 00:01:51.132
There's definitely been an audible gasp

00:01:51.132 --> 00:01:53.386
in the audience once or twice

00:01:53.386 --> 00:01:55.228
while I've walked out on stage.

00:01:55.228 --> 00:01:58.044
(cheering)

00:01:58.044 --> 00:01:59.250
(tapping)

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.098
Tap dancing is very personal.

00:02:01.098 --> 00:02:03.848
It's very much about the emotion.

00:02:05.781 --> 00:02:06.876
(cheering)

00:02:06.876 --> 00:02:08.913
A lot of times you can
tell when a tap dancer

00:02:08.913 --> 00:02:11.663
is happy by the way he's dancing.

00:02:13.712 --> 00:02:15.616
Despite having a diagnosis with cancer,

00:02:15.616 --> 00:02:17.018
I was able to overcome that,

00:02:17.018 --> 00:02:19.080
and I was able to go back to the things

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.809
that I loved the most.

00:02:20.809 --> 00:02:23.163
So, it's always gonna be
a very different emotion

00:02:23.163 --> 00:02:25.580
that is tied to the art form.

00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:29.833
(cheering)

